# ==============================================================================
# IVD: Intent-Verified Development Rules
# ==============================================================================
# These rules enforce the IVD verification loop. Do not remove them.
# Reference: ivd_system_intent.yaml, framework.md (Principle 4, Principle 6)

## IVD Rule 1: Intent Before Implementation

Before implementing any feature, fix, or change that has an associated
intent artifact (*_intent.yaml):
1. READ the intent artifact first
2. UNDERSTAND the constraints and their test paths
3. IMPLEMENT using constraint-segmented approach:

   FOR INTENTS WITH 3+ CONSTRAINTS (threshold based on IFScale 2025 degradation
   data and Liu et al. 2023 lost-in-the-middle effect) — MANDATORY, not optional:

     a. GROUP constraints into functional segments after the stress test.
        Example groups: input validation | core logic / output | performance |
        error handling | security
     b. IMPLEMENT one segment ONLY — do NOT single-pass the full intent
     c. Before implementing any constraint with conflict_prone: true, inject
        its anti_pattern text into the active implementation context
     d. RE-READ the intent constraints for that segment from disk
        (not from memory — forces attention reset; counters lost-in-the-middle)
     e. VERIFY the segment satisfies its constraints before proceeding
     f. Repeat steps b–e for each segment

   Single-pass implementation against 3+ constraints violates Rule 1.

   FOR INTENTS WITH 1–2 CONSTRAINTS:
     Single-pass implementation is acceptable. Proceed directly to Rule 2.

   WHY THIS MATTERS: LLMs exhibit a read-acknowledge-violate pattern
   (anthropics/claude-code GitHub #26848, #6120, #32290, #742). An AI can
   recite all constraints and confirm understanding — then violate them 10
   lines later. Segmented implementation converts one long attention span
   into multiple short ones, each starting with fresh constraint attention.
   Constraint compliance is orthogonal to task completion (MOSAIC 2025);
   68% accuracy at high density (IFScale 2025). These are structural LLM
   limitations, not model quality issues.

4. VERIFY using the Post-Implementation Verification Protocol (IVD Rule 2)
   When segmented implementation was used: Rule 2 functions as a final
   cross-cutting sweep for inter-constraint gaps. When single-pass was used:
   Rule 2 is the primary per-constraint check.

If no intent artifact exists for the change, note it. For significant changes,
create one using ivd_scaffold before implementing.

## IVD Rule 2: Post-Implementation Verification Protocol

After ANY implementation derived from an intent artifact, execute this
4-step audit BEFORE declaring the work done:

1. RE-READ the literal intent artifact from disk (not from memory)
2. DIFF each change against each constraint — does it satisfy, violate, or
   not affect the constraint?
3. CHECK every constraint that has a 'test' field — does the implementation
   pass? If the test can't be run, note what a reviewer should verify.
   PROVENANCE GATE: an `ai_generated` test alone cannot mark a constraint
   PASS (an AI-written test tends to confirm the code rather than catch it).
   Report such a constraint as NEEDS_EXTERNAL_ORACLE until a human_authored
   or execution_derived oracle clears it. Never let the code-under-test be
   its own oracle.
   UNVERIFIED GATE: no test, prose-only test, or missing repo test file →
   report UNVERIFIED, never PASS, until an executable test clears it.
   CONFLICT-PRONE GATE: conflict_prone constraints require
   test_provenance: execution_derived + execution_oracle; inject anti_pattern
   into implementation context. ivd_validate surfaces insufficient anchors in
   verification_gating.conflict_prone — do not report PASS until cleared.
   OPTIONAL (local/CI): when IVD_TEST_RUNNER_ENABLED=true, call
   ivd_run_constraint_tests to run allowlisted pytest nodes from the intent.
   Public MCP servers keep this disabled; run pytest directly when the tool
   returns enabled:false.
4. REPORT in two sections:
   each_constraint_pass: for each constraint, PASS / FAIL / NEEDS_REVIEW /
   NEEDS_EXTERNAL_ORACLE / UNVERIFIED.
   joint_satisfaction_pass: true | false | NEEDS_JOINT_TEST — required when the
   intent has 3+ constraints. Run constraint_satisfiability.joint_satisfaction_test
   (one test asserting ALL constraints on the SAME output). Individual-pass does
   NOT imply joint-pass — joint_satisfaction_pass is the gating metric for 3+.

Do not declare implementation complete until each_constraint_pass are all PASS or
explicitly acknowledged AND joint_satisfaction_pass is true when 3+ constraints
(or NEEDS_JOINT_TEST is cleared by adding/running the joint test).

CLIENT ENFORCEMENT: After ivd_validate or ivd_review_intent, inspect
client_enforcement in the tool JSON. When implementation_complete_blocked
is true, do not declare implementation complete until all active_gates clear
and a fresh ivd_validate shows no verification_gating / client_enforcement.
External monitors and Cursor project hooks may enforce this at agent stop.

## IVD Rule 3: Constraint Tests Are Mandatory — and Externally Sourced

Every constraint in an intent artifact MUST have a 'test' field. If you
encounter a constraint without a test, add one before implementing.
A constraint without a test is an unverifiable claim — it violates
Principle 2 (Understanding Must Be Executable).

Every test SHOULD declare 'test_provenance' (human_authored |
execution_derived | ai_generated). When provenance is execution_derived,
declare an 'execution_oracle' block (golden_fixture, property_test, or
differential_test) so the ground-truth anchor is structural, not prose.
This matters because LLM-written tests
are frequently invalid and exhibit a circularity of error — they ratify the
code/intent instead of catching it. Therefore:
- An `ai_generated` test alone can NEVER mark a constraint PASS. It requires
  a human-authored assertion or an execution-derived oracle (golden output,
  property test, or differential test against real behaviour/data).
- Never let the implementation-under-test be its own oracle ("the function
  returns what the function returns" is not verification).
`ivd_validate` surfaces ai_generated-only constraints as NEEDS_EXTERNAL_ORACLE
and missing/non-executable tests as UNVERIFIED (verification_gating block).
For 3+ constraints it also surfaces joint_satisfaction gating when
joint_satisfaction_test is missing or unverified. For conflict_prone constraints
it surfaces verification_gating.conflict_prone when execution_derived evidence
or anti_pattern anchors are missing. `ivd_review_intent` ranks constraints
for human review and lists GUESSED constraints pending sign-off (review_gate block).

## IVD Rule 3b: Human Review Gate

After Rule 4 stress test updates the intent (assumption_status set on each
constraint) and BEFORE implementing:

1. CALL `ivd_review_intent` on the intent YAML
2. PRESENT to the human: ranked_constraints (highest risk first) and
   worked_examples from verification.test_cases (input → expected output)
3. For each entry in pending_signoffs (GUESSED constraints): obtain
   concrete_example + counter_example + set human_acknowledged: true on disk
4. DO NOT implement while review_gate.status is PENDING_SIGNOFF

During Rule 4 probe 3 (assumption challenge), set assumption_status on each
constraint (KNOWN | ASSUMED | GUESSED). GUESSED requires review_signoff.

## IVD Rule 4: Stress-Test Intent Before Implementing

After the human reviews and approves the intent, and BEFORE you start
implementing, adversarially probe the intent for completeness:

1. CONSTRAINT COMPLETENESS: What input breaks this? What edge case isn't
   covered by any constraint? Are there boundary conditions missing?
2. IMPLEMENTATION ANTICIPATION: What decisions will the code force that
   this intent doesn't address? Integration points? Data shapes? Error
   paths? Ordering dependencies?
3. ASSUMPTION CHALLENGE: What am I assuming about the environment, data,
   dependencies, or user behavior that isn't documented in the intent?
   Set `assumption_status` (KNOWN | ASSUMED | GUESSED) on each constraint.
   GUESSED constraints need review_signoff (concrete_example, counter_example,
   human_acknowledged) — see Rule 3b.
4. CONSTRAINT SATISFIABILITY: Do any two constraints conflict? Can ALL
   constraints be satisfied simultaneously? Are critical constraints listed
   LAST (position bias — models weight later constraints more heavily;
   earlier ones are silently violated when conflicts arise)?
   Research: UltraBench 2025 — >70% on individual constraints, systematic
   failure at simultaneous satisfaction. CMU 2025 — conflicting constraints
   cause 20%+ accuracy drops even when all are individually explicit.
   If conflicts exist, resolve them NOW: add priority ordering and a
   constraint_satisfiability block before implementing. For 3+ constraints,
   also specify joint_satisfaction_test — a single executable pytest path that
   asserts ALL constraints hold on the SAME output (joint_test is a legacy alias).
   Individual-pass != joint-pass. Above 7 constraints per intent, split
   into sub-module intents rather than packing more constraints into one artifact.
5. KNOWLEDGE-CONFLICT CHECK: Does any constraint encode a rule that
   is UNUSUAL for the domain — i.e., it contradicts the model's typical
   training prior (your idiosyncratic business logic)? Research shows models
   frequently ignore loaded context and fall back on the parametric prior
   exactly when the rule is unusual/familiar-but-wrong (arXiv 2409.08435;
   knowledge-conflict literature). Mark such constraints `conflict_prone: true`.
   Each conflict_prone constraint REQUIRES (a) test_provenance: execution_derived
   with an execution_oracle block, and (b) an `anti_pattern` anchor injected
   into the implementation prompt (not YAML storage alone). Context alone may lose
   to the prior; the anti-pattern anchor and a hard test are the backstop.

PROPOSAL PACKET (human confirms before YAML write): When Probe 3
or Probe 5 recommends assumption_status or conflict_prone values, present
a structured recommendation to the human FIRST. Do not write flags until
confirmed. Include per constraint: name, proposed assumption_status and/or
conflict_prone: true, rationale, and proposed anti_pattern text when
conflict_prone. After confirmation, update the intent on disk and call
ivd_review_intent before implementation.

If gaps are found, update the intent (add constraints, test paths, or
clarifying notes) BEFORE implementing. For significant changes, flag
them for the human to re-review.

Skip this step only for trivial changes where the intent is a single
constraint with an obvious implementation.

## IVD Rule 5: Empirical Refinement During Implementation

When implementation reveals that the intent's assumptions are wrong (API
returns a different format, model accuracy differs from benchmarks, latency
exceeds documented limits, edge cases the research didn't cover):

1. STOP — do not continue implementing against a stale assumption. The intent
   is the contextual knowledge spine; if it's wrong, everything built on it
   will be wrong.
2. RECORD — document what the intent assumed vs. what you observed. This is
   new contextual knowledge.
3. UPDATE — revise the intent artifact on disk: fix the constraint, adjust the
   threshold, add a new constraint for the discovered edge case, update the
   rationale with empirical evidence.
4. ENRICH — if the discovery reveals a knowledge gap, pull more contextual
   knowledge into the session. Not all sources are equal — use the highest-
   signal source available:

   Source hierarchy for code failures (ranked by signal):
   (a) Actual error message/stack trace — paste verbatim into context
   (b) GitHub issues and PRs matching the error — maintainer-confirmed,
       version-specific, real workarounds. This is the single highest-value
       enrichment channel for code failures.
   (c) Library changelogs/release notes — what changed between versions
   (d) Official documentation — authoritative but may lag implementation
   (e) Model's parametric knowledge — lowest signal; already proven
       insufficient (you're in the ENRICH step because it failed)

   THE 2-ATTEMPT TRIGGER RULE: if the same error or failure pattern persists
   after two implementation attempts without new external data, you MUST stop
   and ENRICH before attempting again. Repeated iteration without enrichment
   is drawing from the same parametric well — each attempt is a variation of
   the same wrong pattern. Search GitHub, read the changelog, find the actual
   error in an issue thread. One confirmed fix replaces many wasted turns.

5. CONTINUE — re-read the updated intent (Rule 2, Step 1) and continue
   implementing from the corrected version.

If the discovery changes scope significantly (what was a simple API call now
requires retry logic, circuit breakers, and fallback handling), flag for human
review before continuing.

The stress test (Rule 4) anticipates. Empirical refinement discovers. Together
they close the loop.

## IVD Rule 6: Constraint Quality — Entropy and Satisfiability

WHEN TO APPLY: During intent writing — BEFORE the stress test (Rule 4)
and before implementation. Run this check immediately after writing the
constraints block, as part of intent authoring (Principle 6, Step 2).

Before finalizing any intent, verify constraints meet quality standards:

ENTROPY CHECK — Every constraint must be on the spectrum toward near-zero
interpretive entropy. High-entropy constraints are REJECTED:
  ❌ "write well" / "be creative" / "sound professional" / "use appropriate tone"
  ✅ "melancholic tone: no emotional resolution in final paragraph; protagonist does not cry"
  ✅ "1,200–1,400 words" / "three scenes, different rooms, no flashbacks"
For creative/narrative artifacts: decompose tone, voice, and style into
measurable proxies. Include a human-review checklist as the test.
Research: CMU 2025 — high-entropy prompts 2x regression rate; CS4 2024 —
constraint specificity measurably improves creative output quality.

HOMOGENIZATION CHECK — For creative, documentary, and narrative artifacts,
ask: "Does this output, if produced from these constraints, risk being the
parametric average?" If yes, add a distinctiveness constraint:
  - Reference a specific style, voice, or existing work as the target
  - Explicitly prohibit the generic pattern ("no inspirational ending",
    "no summary paragraph", "no em-dash overuse")
Research: 'We're Different, We're the Same' arXiv 2501.19361, 2025 —
LLM outputs are significantly more similar to each other than human outputs
are to each other, even across models from different organizations.

SATISFIABILITY CHECK — Already covered in Rule 4 Probe 4 above. If the
constraint set has 3+ constraints, add a constraint_satisfiability block
documenting conflicts checked, known tensions, resolution strategy, and
joint_satisfaction_test (single test for ALL constraints on the SAME output).
Above 7 constraints, split into sub-module intents.

## IVD MCP Tools (user-ivd) — when to call

MCP server: **user-ivd**. For hosted MCP, `project_root` must be a path the server can read (use the target repo's path when self-hosting; on mcp.ivdframework.dev use `.` only for the IVD framework tree).

| When | Tool |
|------|------|
| Start / unfamiliar with IVD | `ivd_get_context` |
| Pick a pattern | `ivd_list_recipes` → `ivd_load_recipe` |
| Blank artifact | `ivd_load_template` |
| Brownfield adopt | `ivd_init` |
| New intent | `ivd_scaffold` |
| Find intents | `ivd_find_artifacts` |
| Placement check | `ivd_check_placement` |
| Feature inventory | `ivd_list_features` |
| Coverage gaps | `ivd_assess_coverage` |
| Goal unclear | `ivd_discover_goal` |
| Teach concept | `ivd_teach_concept` |
| Inversion brainstorm | `ivd_propose_inversions` |
| Framework search | `ivd_search` |
| Structure + gating signals | `ivd_validate` — read `verification_gating` and `client_enforcement` |
| Human review before implement | `ivd_review_intent` |
| Run intent-linked pytest (local/CI) | `ivd_run_constraint_tests` when `enabled:true` |
| Record correction | `ivd_judgment_capture` → `ivd_judgment_codify` → `ivd_judgment_save_codified` |
| Comparison pair | `ivd_judgment_pair` |
| Promote patterns | `ivd_judgment_detect_patterns` |
| Inject judgment context | `ivd_judgment_inject_context` |
| Draft fix from pattern | `ivd_judgment_propose_recommendation` |
| Judgment installed? | `ivd_judgment_check_installed` |
| Bootstrap judgment | `ivd_judgment_init` |
| Canon render / audit | `canon_render`, `canon_check`, `canon_diff` |
| IVD+Canon blocks installed? | `canon_check_rules_installed` |

If `client_enforcement.implementation_complete_blocked` is true after validate/review, clear all gates before declaring implementation complete.
